::CW_U02_L06_GP_Q01::Passage: "The phone lit up. I reached for it, stopped, and watched my name reflect faintly in the black screen." What pacing choice is used?{
~A. Compression of unimportant events#Incorrect. The passage slows a single important moment rather than compressing it.
~B. A complete summary of a month#Incorrect. The passage focuses on one moment.
=C. Expansion of an important moment#Correct. The writer slows the phone moment with action and visual detail.
~D. No pacing choice#Incorrect. The slowing is a clear pacing choice.
}
::CW_U02_L06_GP_Q02::Which revision creates more tension? Draft: "I opened the door."{
=A. I opened the door slowly because the last text had said, "Do not go inside yet."#Correct. The warning creates stakes and makes the reader wait for what is inside.
~B. I opened the door.#Incorrect. This is unchanged and does not add tension.
~C. Doors are common in buildings.#Incorrect. This is general information, not tension.
~D. The door was a door.#Incorrect. This adds no stakes or pacing.
}
::CW_U02_L06_GP_Q03::Which detail best creates an uneasy mood?{
~A. The hallway was fine.#Incorrect. This tells a vague judgment and does not create mood.
~B. The hallway existed.#Incorrect. This gives no emotional atmosphere.
~C. The hallway was a place.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=D. The hallway light clicked twice, then held steady over the closed doors.#Correct. The sound and image create an uneasy atmosphere.
}
::CW_U02_L06_GP_Q04::What is compression?{
~A. Slowing every moment equally#Incorrect. That is not compression.
=B. Moving quickly through less important time or action#Correct. Compression helps the writer avoid overdeveloping routine events.
~C. Removing all details from important scenes#Incorrect. Important scenes may need expansion.
~D. Changing point of view#Incorrect. Compression is a pacing strategy, not a point-of-view change.
}
::CW_U02_L06_GP_Q05::Which explanation best connects pace to effect?{
~A. The writer uses a long sentence because long sentences are always better.#Incorrect. Sentence length depends on purpose.
~B. The writer adds words for no reason.#Incorrect. Pacing details should have purpose.
=C. The writer slows the latch click with sound and rain detail so the reader waits longer and feels tension.#Correct. This names the pacing choice and explains the effect.
~D. The writer uses tone, so pace does not matter.#Incorrect. Pace and tone can work together.
}
